WEBVTT

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:10.570
<v Ira Glass>Sullivan High School in Chicago is a school where communication isn't the easiest in normal times. Kids constantly have to pass their phones back and forth with Google Translate so they can understand each other.
<v Ira Glass>芝加哥的沙利文高中在平时就是一个沟通并不容易的学校。孩子们必须不断地用谷歌翻译在手机上互相传递，才能理解彼此。

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:19.410
<v Elly Fishman>Over half the kids are immigrants or refugees. They come from close to 40 different countries. They speak over 35 different languages.
<v Elly Fishman>超过一半的孩子是移民或难民。他们来自近40个不同的国家，说着35种以上的不同语言。

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:30.470
<v Ira Glass>That's Elly Fishman, a reporter who spent over three years there and wrote a book about the school called Refugee High. She returned there with one of our producers at the beginning of this school year and was there on the second day of school.
<v Ira Glass>那是艾莉·费什曼，一位在那里花了三年多时间，并写了一本关于这所学校的书《难民高中》的记者。本学年开始时，她和我们的制片人之一回到了那里，并在开学第二天就在现场。

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:45.350
<v Elly Fishman>Kids are really nervous. They're not native English speakers. They don't know each other yet. So they're sitting among a bunch of strangers at this point. So there's a lot of anxiety and nerves.
<v Elly Fishman>孩子们非常紧张。他们的英语不是母语，彼此还不认识。所以此时此刻，他们坐在了一群陌生人中间。因此，这里充满了焦虑和紧张。

00:00:45.350 --> 00:01:07.970
<v Ira Glass>Sullivan High School is organized to make them feel at home. Each step at the staircase by the main entrance says welcome in a different language, private spaces for Muslim students to pray, shelves full of winter coats and backpacks for students who arrive with none, special curriculum and services tailored to them. This year, though, was the first time students were back in person since the pandemic, which posed some special challenges.
<v Ira Glass>沙利文高中旨在让他们感到宾至如归。主入口楼梯的每一级台阶上都用不同的语言写着欢迎，有供穆斯林学生祈祷的私人空间，有为两手空空来到学校的学生准备的装满冬大衣和背包的架子，还有为他们量身定制的特殊课程和服务。然而，今年是疫情以来学生们首次重返学校面对面上课，这也带来了一些特殊的挑战。

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:21.700
<v Ira Glass>Like, for instance, English teacher Annmarie Handley was going around the room student by student doing one of the most pedestrian beginning of school tasks-- filling out the seating chart with the basics. What country are they from? What do they speak besides English?
<v Ira Glass>例如，英语老师安玛丽·汉德利正逐个学生地在教室里走动，做着开学初期最乏味的任务之一——填写座位表的基本信息。他们来自哪个国家？除了英语，他们还会说些什么？

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.870
<v Annmarie Handley>Abdal? What languages do you speak?
<v Annmarie Handley>阿卜杜勒？你会说什么语言？

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:33.320
<v Student>I speak Arabic.
<v Student>我说阿拉伯语。

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.490
<v Annmarie Handley>What languages do you speak, sir?
<v Annmarie Handley>先生，你会说什么语言？

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:57.580
<v Ira Glass>OK. Now, you heard that student perfectly, because our producer was standing right next to him with a microphone just inches from his face. But that long pause, the fact that Ms. Handley asked him the question a second time, that's going to make a lot more sense if I just play you the sound from the microphone that was pinned to Ms. Handley's lapel. This is how she heard that interaction, more or less.
<v Ira Glass>好了。现在，你听得非常清楚，因为我们的制片人就站在他身边，麦克风离他的脸只有几英寸。但是那个长长的停顿，以及汉德利老师第二次问他这个问题的事实，如果我播放别在汉德利老师翻领上的麦克风录下的声音，你们就会更明白了。这大致就是她听到那次互动的方式。

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:11.700
<v Annmarie Handley>Abdal? What languages do you speak? What languages do you speak, sir?
<v Annmarie Handley>阿卜杜勒？你会说什么语言？先生，你会说什么语言？

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:35.940
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. She didn't hear him at all. Because she's way up at the front of the room stuck there typing into a computer. And there's an air purifier that they put in for COVID humming towards the back of the room. And there's an air conditioner running that's in the window just to her right. And most important, everybody is in a mask, muffling the students' voices, and making it so she cannot see anybody's mouth move.
<v Ira Glass>是的。她根本没听见他在说什么。因为她被困在教室最前面打字，而且为了防范新冠病毒，他们在那儿放了一台空气净化器，在教室后面嗡嗡作响。就在她右手边的窗户里，还有一台空调在运行。最重要的是，每个人都戴着口罩，闷住了学生的声音，让她根本看不见任何人的嘴唇动。

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.360
<v Annmarie Handley>Now, you can see how much I normally read lips.
<v Annmarie Handley>现在，你们可以看到我通常有多依赖读唇语了。

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.950
<v Ira Glass>She continues her questions.
<v Ira Glass>她继续提问。

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.630
<v Annmarie Handley>And what country were you born in?
<v Annmarie Handley>你出生在哪个国家？

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.740
<v Student>Egypt.
<v Student>埃及。

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:44.160
<v Annmarie Handley>Where?
<v Annmarie Handley>哪里？

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.480
<v Student>Egypt.
<v Student>埃及。

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.520
<v Annmarie Handley>Asia? What part of Asia?
<v Annmarie Handley>亚洲？亚洲的哪个部分？

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:48.870
<v Student>No, Egypt.
<v Student>不，埃及。

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.230
<v Annmarie Handley>Egypt. Cool.
<v Annmarie Handley>埃及。酷。

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.660
<v Elly Fishman>So she keeps going around the room. And this keeps happening.
<v Elly Fishman>所以她继续在教室里走动。这种情况一直在发生。

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.850
<v Ira Glass>Again, reporter Elly Fishman.
<v Ira Glass>再次提醒，这是记者艾莉·费什曼。

00:02:56.850 --> 00:03:07.290
<v Elly Fishman>It seemed to me, having spent a couple of years in the school before COVID, that it was just exponentially harder. And there was so much more miscommunication.
<v Elly Fishman>对我来说，在新冠疫情前在这所学校待了几年，我觉得这（沟通）简直难上加难。而且误解也多了很多。

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:11.740
<v Annmarie Handley>All right. And then we have Claude. What languages do you speak?
<v Annmarie Handley>好了。接下来是克劳德。你会说什么语言？

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.650
<v Student>What say?
<v Student>你说什么？

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.330
<v Annmarie Handley>What languages do you speak?
<v Annmarie Handley>你会说什么语言？

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:20.340
<v Student>Kinyarwanda. Kinyarwanda.
<v Student>卢旺达语。卢旺达语。

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.960
<v Annmarie Handley>And what country were you born in?
<v Annmarie Handley>你出生在哪个国家？

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:22.960
<v Student>Congo.
<v Student>刚果。

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.350
<v Annmarie Handley>Rwanda.
<v Annmarie Handley>卢旺达。

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:27.040
<v Student>I said Congo.
<v Student>我说的是刚果。

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:27.600
<v Annmarie Handley>One more, table.
<v Annmarie Handley>再问一个，桌子。

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:28.320
<v Student>Dang.
<v Student>该死。

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:29.320
<v Annmarie Handley>Huh?
<v Annmarie Handley>哈？

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:30.820
<v Student>I said Congo.
<v Student>我说的是刚果。

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:31.420
<v Annmarie Handley>Congo.
<v Annmarie Handley>刚果。

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:31.810
<v Student>Uh-huh.
<v Student>嗯哼。

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:38.450
<v Annmarie Handley>Oh my gosh. I'm so sorry. I thought you said Rwanda. I will fix it.
<v Annmarie Handley>天哪。真抱歉。我以为你说的是卢旺达。我会改过来的。

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:55.800
<v Ira Glass>It's painful to listen to-- hearing these students give the teacher exactly what she's asking for. And she can't tell. Everybody is trying to connect. And they are not connecting. In the recording, you can hear Ms. Handley try to manage this awkwardness, save the students from the embarrassment, turn it all on herself.
<v Ira Glass>听着这些太让人难受了——听到这些学生给老师提供了她所要求的确切信息，但她却听不出来。每个人都在试图建立联系，但他们却无法沟通。在录音中，你可以听到汉德利老师试图处理这种尴尬，挽救学生的难堪，把一切过错揽在自己身上。

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:03.870
<v Annmarie Handley>Sorry. It's the air conditioner. It's not you guys. Your English is good. It's just this is right in my ear. And it's killing me.
<v Annmarie Handley>抱歉。是空调的原因。不是你们。你们的英语很好。只是这声音就在我耳边。简直要了我的命。

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:11.940
<v Ira Glass>When I reached Ms. Handley, she said, it is a lot harder to conduct this class without being able to see the kids' mouths. But for her, it really just feels like one more thing to deal with.
<v Ira Glass>当我联系到汉德利老师时，她说，无法看到孩子们的嘴巴确实让上课变得困难多了。但对她来说，这感觉就像是又多了一件要处理的事情。

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:30.800
<v Annmarie Handley>I think I worry more that the kid is getting frustrated, because especially at the beginning of the year, I need them to know that I'm listening and they can trust me. And I don't want them to feel as though I'm not listening or I don't care.
<v Annmarie Handley>我想我更担心的是孩子会感到沮丧，因为特别是在学年初，我需要让他们知道我在听，他们可以信任我。我不希望他们觉得我没有在听，或者我不关心他们。

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:46.610
<v Ira Glass>She says in the months since then, it's gotten a lot easier to communicate. The air conditioning is off. And more important, everybody is more comfortable with each other and with her, which means they're more comfortable speaking up, projecting, making themselves heard through the mask.
<v Ira Glass>她说，从那以后的几个月里，沟通变得容易多了。空调关了。更重要的是，大家彼此之间以及与她相处得更融洽了，这意味着他们更愿意开口说话、大声表达，让别人能透过口罩听到他们的声音。

00:04:46.610 --> 00:05:06.230
<v Ira Glass>Communication is delicate. And if you don't get it right, it could be disastrous. They got through it here in Ms. Handley's class. But in our other stories today, it does not go so well. Like in one story, the future of democracy is at stake. In another, a mom tries to say something important to her own daughter so ineffectively.
<v Ira Glass>沟通是很微妙的。如果你处理得不好，可能会导致灾难性的后果。在汉德利老师的课堂上，他们挺过来了。但在我们今天讲述的其他故事里，情况就不那么好了。比如在一个故事里，民主的未来岌岌可危。在另一个故事里，一位母亲试图对她自己的女儿说些重要的事情，却如此徒劳。

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:27.530
<v Ira Glass>Also, Amelia Bedelia-- remember her-- who hears, but never understands. Where is she today? Here. From WBEZ Chicago, this is This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>还有，阿米莉亚·贝迪莉亚——还记得她吗——她听得见，但从不理解。她今天在哪里？在这里。来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:48.540
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Until The Cows Come Home. So this first story is about a man trying to communicate a thing, a very particular thing-- that the 2020 election in the state of Michigan was not stolen, that Joe Biden won. This is not an outcome this man is particularly happy about. He's a Republican, a state senator. But he says, this is the truth.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，直到母牛回家。第一个故事讲的是一个男人试图传达一件事，一件非常具体的事情——2020年密歇根州的大选并没有被窃取，乔·拜登赢了。这不是一个让这个男人特别开心的结果。他是一名共和党人，州参议员。但他坚持认为，这是事实。

00:05:48.540 --> 00:06:02.400
<v Ira Glass>The man's name is Ed McBroom. He chaired the official state committee that looked into all the claims of election fraud in Michigan. In the end, they wrote a report concluding that there was, quote, "no evidence of widespread or systematic fraud."
<v Ira Glass>那个男人的名字叫埃德·麦克布鲁姆。他担任了官方州委员会的主席，调查了密歇根州所有关于选举舞弊的指控。最终，他们写了一份报告，结论是，引用：“没有证据表明存在广泛或系统的舞弊。”

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:27.830
<v Ira Glass>Lots of his fellow Republicans do not accept this conclusion. And they are not shy about telling him that when they run into him. So Ed has been on a kind of personal campaign to talk to anybody who doubts him, as long as they want, in the hopes of being able to give them the facts they need to reach the same conclusion that he did. Our senior editor David Kestenbaum wondered if he was persuading anybody, and a few months ago went up to spend a day with Ed at the Upper Peninsula State Fair.
<v Ira Glass>他的许多共和党同僚并不接受这个结论。当他们遇到他时，他们一点也不避讳地告诉他。所以埃德一直在进行一种个人的“竞选活动”，去和任何质疑他的人交谈，只要他们愿意，希望能提供他们需要的事实，让他们得出和他一样的结论。我们的高级编辑大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆想知道他是否说服了任何人，几个月前他去上半岛州博览会和埃德待了一天。

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:35.640
<v Ira Glass>Ed promised, from out in the open for five minutes with my cows, somebody will come up and talk about the election. Here's David.
<v Ira Glass>埃德承诺，只要在外面和我的奶牛待五分钟，就会有人上来谈论选举。这是大卫的报道。

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:44.250
<v David Kestenbaum>I met Ed in the dairy barn at the state fair with his cows and his kids. That's kind of how he's known, the state senator with the cows and kids.
<v David Kestenbaum>我在州博览会的奶牛棚里见到了埃德，他带着他的奶牛和孩子们。这就是他为人所知的方式，那位带着奶牛和孩子的州参议员。

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:49.590
<v Ed McBroom>Helen, Melody, Eddie, Carl, Esther--
<v Ed McBroom>海伦、梅洛迪、埃迪、卡尔、埃丝特——

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:52.740
<v David Kestenbaum>These are the kids, not the cows. There are more.
<v David Kestenbaum>这些是孩子，不是奶牛。还有更多。

00:06:52.740 --> 00:07:03.780
<v Ed McBroom>Brita, Royal, Odell, Oriana, Kenny-Jack, Edith, Magnus, and Sayla. So there's 13 of them.
<v Ed McBroom>布丽塔、罗亚尔、奥德尔、奥里亚娜、肯尼-杰克、伊迪丝、马格努斯和塞拉。总共有13个。

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:09.090
<v David Kestenbaum>You want to say a word about what that life is like? If you had to pick one word.
<v David Kestenbaum>你想用一个词来描述这种生活是什么样的吗？如果你必须选一个词。

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:18.960
<v Ed McBroom>One word-- I think I'm going to say blessed, I guess. Blessed or hard-- blessed or hard.
<v Ed McBroom>一个词——我想我会说是“蒙福的”，我想。蒙福或艰苦——蒙福或艰苦。

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:27.180
<v David Kestenbaum>Five of the kids are Ed's. The other eight were his brother's, who died three years ago and lived next door. So the families were always kind of one unit.
<v David Kestenbaum>其中五个孩子是埃德的。另外八个是他三年前去世、住在隔壁的兄弟的孩子。所以这两个家庭一直就像一个整体。

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:45.030
<v David Kestenbaum>There's one photo I saw on Ed's Facebook page of a kid who had put together some delightfully crazy outfit. But the thing that stood out to me was the stuff in the background of the photo, total chaos in the kitchen behind her. Ed's a farmer, a parent, and a state senator. He says sometimes, it's a bit much to handle.
<v David Kestenbaum>我在埃德的脸书页面上看到一张照片，是一个孩子穿了一套令人愉悦的疯狂服装。但最吸引我的是照片背景里的东西，她身后的厨房简直是一片混乱。埃德是一个农民、家长和州参议员。他说，有时这确实难以应付。

00:07:45.030 --> 00:08:17.040
<v David Kestenbaum>I was interested in Ed, because honestly, it's alarming to me all the people who still believe the election was stolen. In one poll, a third of Americans and most Republicans. It's bad enough that we as a country seem completely divided on everything-- the climate, vaccines, immigration. But somehow, this idea that so many people look at the official election results certified by the states-- the method we have for settling all these disputes-- and say, that's a lie. That feels particularly unsettling.
<v David Kestenbaum>我对埃德很感兴趣，因为老实说，对于所有仍然相信选举被窃取的人，这让我感到惊恐。在一项民意调查中，三分之一的美国人和大多数共和党人这样认为。我们这个国家在任何事情上似乎都彻底分裂了，这已经够糟糕了——气候、疫苗、移民。但不知何故，这么多人看着各州认证的官方选举结果——我们解决所有这些争端的方法——却说，那是谎言。这感觉特别令人不安。

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:37.740
<v David Kestenbaum>And so here was Ed, this guy in the trenches, arguing for the facts in a way that few people are. I wanted to see how that was going and what the conversations were like. Ed ventures out of the dairy barn, where his cows are, past the fair games and the rides, to this indoor area where various vendors are set up, and where the Republican Party has a table.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以埃德就在这里，这个身处战壕中的家伙，以很少有人使用的方式为事实进行辩论。我想看看进展如何，对话是什么样的。埃德走出奶牛所在的奶牛棚，经过博览会的游乐设施，来到这个室内区域，那里设有各种摊位，共和党也有一个桌子。

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.150
<v Ed McBroom>Hello, ma'am. Hi.
<v Ed McBroom>你好，夫人。嗨。

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.550
<v Donna>Hi. I was at Cityfest last week--
<v Donna>嗨。我上周在城市节时——

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:42.300
<v Ed McBroom>Oh.
<v Ed McBroom>哦。

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:57.060
<v David Kestenbaum>People stop by who know him to catch up. A woman from the Gideon's Bible table across the way, comes over to say that they've been praying for him. And then this man and a woman come over. The first thing she says is, why aren't you having a full forensic audit?
<v David Kestenbaum>认识他的人会停下来和他叙旧。街对面基甸圣经摊位的一位女士走过来，说他们一直在为他祈祷。然后，这个男人和一个女人走了过来。她说的第一句话是，你们为什么不进行全面的法医审计？

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:06.380
<v David Kestenbaum>Forensic audit is the battle cry for people who want some external group to look at the ballots and everything, which for the record, have already been through a state-led, bipartisan audit in Michigan.
<v David Kestenbaum>法医审计是那些想要外部组织查看选票及一切事物的人们的战斗口号，顺便说一下，这些东西在密歇根州已经经过了州领导的两党审计。

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:10.380
<v Donna>So why aren't you having a full forensic audit?
<v Donna>那么你们为什么不进行全面的法医审计？

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:21.750
<v Dan>You need one, badly. Those two vans that went in the FTC center in the middle of the night with nobody watching except the cameras-- and I saw it on Gateway Pundit. I know they went in there. And the little black car phone--
<v Dan>你们非常需要。那两辆在深夜进入联邦贸易委员会中心、除了摄像头外没人看守的货车——我在“盖特韦专家”网站上看到了。我知道他们进去了。还有那辆黑色的小车电话——

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:25.770
<v Ed McBroom>Well, nobody's denying that the vans came. It's just that we know what was in them--
<v Ed McBroom>嗯，没有人否认货车来了。只是我们知道里面装的是什么——

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:26.700
<v Dan>Bull--
<v Dan>胡扯——

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:27.630
<v Donna>Dan, stop.
<v Donna>丹，住口。

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:29.070
<v Ed McBroom>Well, I mean, the facts are--
<v Ed McBroom>嗯，我是说，事实是——

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:30.060
<v Dan>You do not know--
<v Dan>你根本不知道——

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:30.480
<v Ed McBroom>Well, sure we do.
<v Ed McBroom>嗯，当然我们知道。

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:36.150
<v Dan>Did you see every ballot that was in there and see that it was folded, if it was mail-in? Did you see that it was signed?
<v Dan>你看到里面的每一张选票了吗，如果是邮寄选票，你看到它是折叠的了吗？你看到它是签过字的吗？

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:36.990
<v Ed McBroom>So listen--
<v Ed McBroom>所以听着——

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:38.100
<v Dan>Did you check any of this?
<v Dan>你检查过这些吗？

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.500
<v Ed McBroom>So listen to the explanation, though. I mean, because--
<v Ed McBroom>所以听听解释，好吗？我是说，因为——

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:57.090
<v David Kestenbaum>Ed's approach to these conversations is to answer whatever questions people have in detail, however long it takes, which is premised on an idea that I hope is true, but I kind of fear isn't. Basically, if you calmly present people with facts, they'll consider them.
<v David Kestenbaum>埃德处理这些对话的方法是详细回答人们提出的任何问题，无论花费多长时间，这基于一个我希望是真的、但我又有点担心不是真的想法。基本上，如果你冷静地向人们展示事实，他们会考虑这些事实的。

00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:10.020
<v David Kestenbaum>The thing the man is asking Ed about is something that got a lot of attention online, a video of a van arriving at the convention center in Detroit after 3:00 in the morning with a bunch of ballots. Seemed suspicious to them.
<v David Kestenbaum>那个人问埃德的事情是网上引起很多关注的事情，一段凌晨3点后一辆货车到达底特律会议中心、装有一堆选票的视频。对他们来说这看起来很可疑。

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:25.110
<v David Kestenbaum>Ed lays out what his committee found. Those were ballots that had been placed in drop boxes, which he points out he's not a fan of drop boxes. But he says he looked into it. And the ballots were just going through the normal procedure, though the van didn't have any particular security.
<v David Kestenbaum>埃德列出了他委员会发现的情况。那些是投入投递箱的选票，他指出他并不喜欢投递箱。但他说是他调查过了。选票只是经过了正常的程序，虽然那辆货车没有任何特别的安保。

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:31.770
<v Ed McBroom>So then the question to me is well, could somebody have done something to those ballots on the way there?
<v Ed McBroom>所以问题对我来说就是，嗯，有人在运送途中对那些选票做了手脚吗？

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:47.620
<v David Kestenbaum>Could the driver have been in on it? Switched to a different van with fraudulent ballots? It would have been super hard, he tells them. The ballots would have to have been prepared in advance. The identifying numbers on the ballots and the addresses on them would have to match up with real voters who hadn't voted elsewhere.
<v David Kestenbaum>司机可能参与其中了吗？换成了装有欺诈选票的另一辆车？他告诉他们，那将非常困难。选票必须提前准备好。选票上的识别码和地址必须与没有在其他地方投过票的真实选民相符。

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:59.850
<v Ed McBroom>And so that's a lot of crazy rigmarole. And in the end, what are they going to gain when they know that in Detroit, these absentee ballots are going to run 95% for Joe Biden?
<v Ed McBroom>所以那需要很多疯狂的繁琐程序。最终，当他们知道在底特律，这些缺席选票有95%会投给乔·拜登时，他们能得到什么？

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:00.780
<v Dan>No way.
<v Dan>没门。

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:01.600
<v Ed McBroom>They always do.
<v Ed McBroom>他们总是这样。

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:03.760
<v Dan>No way. They didn't the last election.
<v Dan>没门。上次选举他们没有这样。

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:04.700
<v Ed McBroom>Sure they did.
<v Ed McBroom>他们当然有。

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.150
<v Dan>Not 95%.
<v Dan>不是95%。

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:07.770
<v Ed McBroom>The absentee ballots in Detroit?
<v Ed McBroom>底特律的缺席选票？

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:08.850
<v Dan>Oh, absentee.
<v Dan>哦，缺席选票。

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:17.370
<v Ed McBroom>They tend to run very high for the Democrat side. Trump did better than any Republican for 70 years. And so--
<v Ed McBroom>它们往往会有极高比例投给民主党。特朗普的表现比70年来任何一位共和党人都好。所以——

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:34.560
<v David Kestenbaum>Trump won Michigan, the guy said there. Ed told me in the days after the election, he started out with the same questions these two people have. There were lots of calls into his office after the election. And it wasn't like he automatically assumed there was fraud. But there was stuff he definitely felt deserved looking into.
<v David Kestenbaum>那人说，特朗普赢了密歇根。埃德告诉我，在选举后的几天里，他开始时也和这两个人有同样的疑问。选举后他的办公室接到了很多电话。他并非自动假设存在舞弊。但确实有一些他觉得值得调查的事情。

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:50.850
<v Ed McBroom>Why am I seeing these news reports of chaos at a polling place? Why am I hearing about late night deliveries? Why did one county here flip so dramatically from one candidate to the other? Why are there rumors of overseas--
<v Ed McBroom>为什么我看到关于投票站混乱的新闻报道？为什么我听到关于深夜送货的消息？为什么这里的一个县从一个候选人倒向另一个候选人的转变如此剧烈？为什么会有海外的传言——

00:11:50.850 --> 00:12:00.940
<v David Kestenbaum>It was a long list. Ed thought, well, I can try to figure this stuff out. He heads the Michigan Senate Oversight committee. So he started an investigation.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是一个很长的清单。埃德想，好吧，我可以试着弄明白这些事情。他是密歇根州参议院监督委员会的主席。所以他展开了调查。

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:11.280
<v David Kestenbaum>Anyone who had anything to say could testify. The hearings went on for months. And Ed got way into the details. What if someone was given a Sharpie to mark their ballot and it bled through?
<v David Kestenbaum>任何有话要说的人都可以作证。听证会持续了几个月。埃德深入研究了细节。如果有人拿到记号笔去标记选票，笔墨渗透了怎么办？

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:16.830
<v David Kestenbaum>How does the election management software work? What happens if someone runs a bunch of ballots through the counter twice?
<v David Kestenbaum>选举管理软件是如何工作的？如果有人把一堆选票在计数器上过了两次会发生什么？

00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:27.660
<v Ed McBroom>That took time for me to learn. And then I had to verify that what I learned was accurate, because you don't want to just take one source as authoritative. I wanted to check others.
<v Ed McBroom>这花了我很长时间去学习。然后我必须验证我所学的是准确的，因为你不想只把一个来源当作权威。我想检查其他的。

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:36.870
<v David Kestenbaum>It took way longer than he'd thought. He'd run down one theory. But then someone would say, well, what if it happened this way? So he'd look into that.
<v David Kestenbaum>这花费的时间比他预想的要长得多。他会逐个驳斥一个理论。但然后会有人说，嗯，如果发生这种情况呢？所以他会去调查。

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:53.700
<v Ed McBroom>As the original ideas started to fall to the wayside, I did begin to grow less and less suspicious that I was going to find anything that was going to overturn the results of the election.
<v Ed McBroom>随着最初的想法开始被抛弃，我确实开始越来越不怀疑我会找到任何能够推翻选举结果的东西。

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:57.980
<v David Kestenbaum>How much time did you spend looking into it do you think, all told?
<v David Kestenbaum>总共算下来，你花了多少时间去调查？

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:04.250
<v Ed McBroom>Oh, I don't know. It was hundreds of hours.
<v Ed McBroom>哦，我不知道。那是数百个小时。

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:19.430
<v David Kestenbaum>Ed put together a 35-page report with a 20-page appendix. He lays out each of the theories and really why they can't be true. When the report came out, both Trump and Obama tweeted about it. Actually, Trump issued a statement, since he'd been banned from Twitter.
<v David Kestenbaum>埃德整理了一份35页的报告，外加20页的附录。他列出了每一个理论，并阐述了为什么它们不可能成立。报告出炉时，特朗普和奥巴马都发推文评论了它。事实上，特朗普发表了一份声明，因为他被推特封禁了。

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:27.080
<v David Kestenbaum>Ed says he doesn't imagine either of the former presidents actually read it. Dan and Donna, that's their names, did not seem like they had read it.
<v David Kestenbaum>埃德说，他不认为两位前总统真的读过它。丹和唐娜，那是他们的名字，看起来并不像是读过它。

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:46.540
<v Donna>What I want to tell you is that I know you have children. We have seven children. We have 17 grandchildren. And if we don't straighten this out now in 2021-- I don't like Biden. I like Trump. But that has nothing to do with it at all-- our children will never have a free election, if this communism, socialism crap--
<v Donna>我想告诉你的是，我知道你有孩子。我们有七个孩子。我们有17个孙辈。如果我们现在不在2021年把这件事弄清楚——我不喜欢拜登。我喜欢特朗普。但这根本无关紧要——我们的孩子将永远不会有自由的选举，如果这种共产主义、社会主义的垃圾——

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:47.360
<v Dan>What is so wrong with an audit?
<v Dan>审计到底有什么错？

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:48.290
<v Donna>Because-- you know, because listen.
<v Donna>因为——你知道，因为听着。

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:49.270
<v Dan>Then you could prove Biden won.
<v Dan>那你就可以证明拜登赢了。

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:50.420
<v Ed McBroom>What is the question you started with?
<v Ed McBroom>你一开始的问题是什么？

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.290
<v Dan>And he should be one--
<v Dan>而且他应该是那个——

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:57.050
<v Ed McBroom>That's the question you started with. So for one thing, the state has done two audits.
<v Ed McBroom>那是你一开始的问题。所以首先，州政府已经进行了两次审计。

00:13:57.050 --> 00:14:00.120
<v Dan>Not thorough forensic ones like Arizona did.
<v Dan>不像亚利桑那州那样进行彻底的法医审计。

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:01.340
<v Ed McBroom>So I'll tell you--
<v Ed McBroom>所以我告诉你——

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:09.920
<v David Kestenbaum>Again, Ed goes into all the details. The state had already done a thorough audit, where they recounted a sample of paper ballots by hand from all over the state.
<v David Kestenbaum>再次重申，埃德深入到了所有细节。州政府已经进行了一次彻底的审计，他们手工重新计票了全州各地纸质选票的样本。

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:19.730
<v Ed McBroom>When we go and recount these precincts by hand, we don't find a single one of them to be out of line. Not one of them is out of line. And we did this audit--
<v Ed McBroom>当我们手工重新计票这些选区时，我们没有发现其中任何一个不合规。没有一个是不合规的。我们进行了这次审计——

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:20.240
<v Dan>So both--
<v Dan>所以两者——

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:34.150
<v Ed McBroom>Actually, a much more substantive audit than just that, because in that audit process, they don't just hand recount the ballots. But they're also checking the signatures. They're checking the applications. They're checking the paperwork for the testing. They're checking the test decks that go in.
<v Ed McBroom>实际上，这是一次比仅仅手工重新计票选票更实质性的审计，因为在那个审计过程中，他们不仅手工重新计票。他们还在检查签名。他们在检查申请。他们在检查测试的文书工作。他们在检查投入的测试堆。

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:36.140
<v Dan>Who is doing the checking?
<v Dan>是谁在检查？

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.450
<v Ed McBroom>So the clerk's office.
<v Ed McBroom>书记官办公室。

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:39.680
<v Dan>And are they all Democrats?
<v Dan>他们全都是民主党人吗？

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:48.470
<v Ed McBroom>No. All over the state. You have more Republican clerks than you have Democrat clerks all over the state. And most of the counties have multiple parties.
<v Ed McBroom>不是。全州各地都是。在全州范围内，共和党书记官比民主党书记官多。而且大多数县都有多个党派。

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:56.150
<v Ed McBroom>So you have your county clerk within your townships. You'll have Democrats and Republicans. And so these people are all looking at each other.
<v Ed McBroom>所以你在你的镇区里有你的县书记官。你会同时有民主党人和共和党人。所以这些人都在互相监督。

00:14:56.150 --> 00:15:09.680
<v David Kestenbaum>He doesn't seem to be making much headway. The problem Ed runs into all the time is that he'll carefully build this wall out of blocks of facts. But the person he's talking to will just pivot to another claim, which is exactly what happens next.
<v David Kestenbaum>他似乎并没有取得多大进展。埃德总是遇到的问题是，他会小心翼翼地用事实砌成这堵墙。但与他交谈的人只会转向另一个说法，而这正是接下来发生的事情。

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:11.670
<v Dan>I'm just wondering what was wrong with doing a full audit?
<v Dan>我只是想知道，进行全面审计有什么问题？

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.140
<v Ed McBroom>Well, and I'm glad you asked. I mean--
<v Ed McBroom>嗯，很高兴你问了。我是说——

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.530
<v Dan>I mean, of the machines too.
<v Dan>我是说，对机器也一样。

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:15.100
<v Ed McBroom>Right.
<v Ed McBroom>对。

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:16.790
<v Dan>And did they change the batteries in the machines?
<v Dan>他们换了机器里的电池了吗？

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.950
<v Ed McBroom>So the machines that they changed the battery in--
<v Ed McBroom>所以他们更换电池的那些机器——

00:15:18.950 --> 00:15:33.620
<v David Kestenbaum>The idea here is that the batteries had been changed in some of the voting machines, causing them to erase data. Ed tells them, the machines with the batteries in them were just machines that marked ballots for people with disabilities, not ballot counting machines.
<v David Kestenbaum>这里的想法是，一些投票机的电池被更换了，导致它们删除了数据。埃德告诉他们，那些带有电池的机器只是为残疾人标记选票的机器，不是计票机。

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:43.290
<v David Kestenbaum>You sound so calm in a lot of this. Are you feeling calm? You've said these sentences so many times. Is there some part of you that is tired of it?
<v David Kestenbaum>你在处理很多这些问题时听起来非常冷静。你感觉冷静吗？这些句子你已经说了无数遍。有没有哪部分是你觉得厌倦的？

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:56.480
<v Ed McBroom>There's a bit of a exhaustion, I suppose. But no. I mean, I feel very-- I don't know if it's compassionate or just passionate about helping people find the truth. I wanted the truth.
<v Ed McBroom>我想确实有一点精疲力竭。但没有。我是说，我觉得非常——我不知道这是同情还是仅仅是对帮助人们找到真相充满热情。我想要真相。

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:08.360
<v Ed McBroom>I looked hard. I have resources not every person has because of my position. I used them. Now, I want to give the benefit that I have found to other people.
<v Ed McBroom>我仔细看了。由于我的职位，我有其他人没有的资源。我使用了它们。现在，我希望把我的发现分享给其他人。

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:15.050
<v David Kestenbaum>I feel like that's the perfect answer. I guess I'm asking if you actually always feel that way?
<v David Kestenbaum>我觉得这是一个完美的答案。我猜我是想问你是不是真的总是那样感觉？

00:16:15.050 --> 00:16:18.330
<v Ed McBroom>I would say 99% of the time.
<v Ed McBroom>我会说99%的时候都是。

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:28.040
<v David Kestenbaum>Ed's been talking to this couple for about 25 minutes at this point. He doesn't seem to be moving them. But again, this is his strategy, answer every question they have.
<v David Kestenbaum>埃德此时已经和这对夫妇谈了大约25分钟了。他似乎并没有动摇他们。但再说一次，这是他的策略，回答他们提出的每一个问题。

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:38.870
<v David Kestenbaum>Then something happens though that throws a wrench in this whole thing. By now, a small crowd of people has gathered around. And one guy who's been watching jumps in. His first word-- baloney.
<v David Kestenbaum>但随后发生了一件事，给这一切泼了一盆冷水。到现在，一小群人聚集在周围。一个一直在观看的家伙跳了出来。他的第一个词——胡扯。

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:40.480
<v Ed McBroom>--have proved themselves less than trustworthy.
<v Ed McBroom>——已经证明了自己不值得信任。

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:42.620
<v Man 1>Baloney.
<v Man 1>胡扯。

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:47.600
<v David Kestenbaum>He tells Ed he doesn't understand why they can't have an independent audit. He's pretty insistent.
<v David Kestenbaum>他告诉埃德，他不明白为什么他们不能进行独立的审计。他非常坚持。

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:48.590
<v Man 1>Audits should be done for everything.
<v Man 1>凡事都应该进行审计。

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:50.410
<v Ed McBroom>We've don a very substantial audit.
<v Ed McBroom>我们已经进行了非常实质性的审计。

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:59.420
<v Man 1>We counted the same garbage that was there in the first place. OK, why were the elections in six states closed off at 3:00 in the morning and only to resume at 8:00, when that's never happened before?
<v Man 1>我们重新计算的只是当初就在那里的同样的垃圾。好了，为什么六个州的选举在凌晨3点被封锁，直到8点才恢复，而这以前从未发生过？

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:00.050
<v Ed McBroom>It didn't happen in Michigan.
<v Ed McBroom>密歇根州没有发生这种情况。

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:01.300
<v Man 1>It certainly did in Michigan.
<v Man 1>密歇根州确实发生了。

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:05.810
<v Ed McBroom>Absolutely did not. That's a lie. People are lying to you. It didn't happen.
<v Ed McBroom>绝对没有。那是谎言。人们在对你撒谎。它没有发生。

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:22.580
<v David Kestenbaum>Now Ed says, it's no longer just him having a conversation with this couple. It's like a live version of what happens on social media, and I think why things go so badly there. Ed's trying to have one conversation, and feels like he's maybe getting somewhere. And then this disruptor comes in from the side.
<v David Kestenbaum>现在埃德说，这不再只是他和这对夫妇之间的对话了。这就像社交媒体上发生事情的直播版，我觉得这就是为什么那里进展如此糟糕的原因。埃德试图进行一次对话，并觉得可能有所进展。然后这个捣乱者从侧面切入。

00:17:22.580 --> 00:17:26.420
<v Ed McBroom>And you still had Democrat and Republican poll workers there the whole time.
<v Ed McBroom>而且你整个期间都有民主党和共和党的投票工作人员在场。

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:35.360
<v Man 1>Sounds like you convinced yourself, but I know what I saw. I can't get past the fact that there's no fraud to be seen. Really, Ed?
<v Man 1>听起来你是说服了你自己，但我知道我看到了什么。我无法跨过没有舞弊这个事实。真的吗，埃德？

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:36.890
<v Ed McBroom>I didn't say that. The report doesn't--
<v Ed McBroom>我没那么说。报告没有——

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:56.060
<v David Kestenbaum>Ed later learned that this guy-- he's the son of the couple who was talking to Ed. Ed doesn't convince any of them. Dan, the dad who'd come up with his wife at the beginning, who Ed hoped to make headway with said he just didn't think Ed had the expertise to evaluate the election. And what are you going to say to that?
<v David Kestenbaum>埃德后来得知，这个家伙——他是和埃德交谈的那对夫妇的儿子。埃德没有说服他们中的任何一个。在开始时和妻子一起过来、埃德希望有所进展的父亲丹说，他只是认为埃德没有评估选举的专业知识。你对此有什么好说的？

00:17:56.060 --> 00:18:15.620
<v David Kestenbaum>A whole half hour of careful effort on Ed's part to lead them down a logical path is undercut in a sentence. He's trying, Dan said, I'll give him that. He's trying his best.
<v David Kestenbaum>埃德花了整整半小时的小心努力来引导他们走上一条逻辑道路，却在一句话中被推翻了。他说，他正在尝试，我承认这一点。他在尽他最大的努力。

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:30.590
<v David Kestenbaum>I watched Ed talk to one more woman. She came up after the others have gone. She was middle aged and full of questions. She brought up almost an exact list of things the others did-- the van in the middle of the night, dead people voting, whether the voting machines could have been hacked.
<v David Kestenbaum>我看埃德又和一个女人谈了话。她在其他人离开后走上前来。她中年，充满了疑问。她提出了和别人几乎完全一样的清单——深夜的货车、死人投票、投票机是否可能被黑。

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:35.930
<v Woman 1>You're saying the modems, or the computers were not connected to the internet?
<v Woman 1>你是说，调制解调器，或者电脑没有连接到互联网？

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:51.830
<v David Kestenbaum>The conversation feels like it's going exactly like the other conversation. They talk about the hacking possibility for a long time. But then they talk about Afghanistan and the price of electricity in the Upper Peninsula, which they agree on. It's expensive.
<v David Kestenbaum>对话感觉和之前的对话一模一样。他们谈论了很久黑客的可能性。但随后他们谈论了阿富汗和上半岛的电价，他们对此达成了共识。太贵了。

00:18:51.830 --> 00:19:17.360
<v David Kestenbaum>This time, they don't get interrupted by anyone else. It's just two people talking at the state fair, which I think maybe more than Ed's laying out all the facts, is the reason why this might work. If he can put in enough time with someone, be with them long enough, they get a sense for him. There's a power in physically being in the same space as another person. They're real in a way that someone online or on TV isn't.
<v David Kestenbaum>这次，他们没有被其他人打断。这只是州博览会上两个人的交谈，我想这可能比埃德罗列所有事实更重要，这就是为什么这可能有效的原因。如果他能花足够的时间陪伴某人，和他们在一起足够久，他们就会对那个人产生感觉。在物理空间上和另一个人在一起有一种力量。他们是真实的，不像网上或电视上的人。

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:23.600
<v David Kestenbaum>Finally, they circle back to the election from so many months ago. Here's how the conversation ends.
<v David Kestenbaum>最后，他们回到了几个月前的选举话题。对话是这样结束的。

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:29.480
<v Woman 1>If something comes up, I hope you will support an audit, forensic audit.
<v Woman 1>如果有任何事情出现，我希望你能支持审计，法医审计。

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:41.210
<v Ed McBroom>To look for any new compelling issue that we can't answer with the information we have-- I'm all ears to hear it. I'm curious too. I want answers.
<v Ed McBroom>为了寻找任何我们无法用现有信息回答的新令人信服的问题——我洗耳恭听。我也很好奇。我想要答案。

00:19:41.210 --> 00:20:08.790
<v Ed McBroom>But all the issues they bring forward are either flagrantly impossible or issues that have already dealt with to a deep dive extent. And I just keep on asking them, show me where I'm wrong. And rather than do that, they just call me names or say I'm a bad person or paid off by China and shoot the messenger. And I'm like, please, shoot the message for me, because that would help me then go forward and find out what I did wrong.
<v Ed McBroom>但他们提出的所有问题要么是公然不可能的，要么是已经深入研究过的问题。而我只是不断地要求他们，指出我哪里错了。而他们没有那样做，只是给我贴标签，或者说我是坏人，或者被中国收买了，然后迁怒于送信人。而我就像，拜托，请对我传达的信息进行质疑，因为那将有助于我向前看，找出我哪里错了。

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:15.620
<v Ed McBroom>But I don't-- they don't do that. And it's frustrating because I'm just as curious as you are about it.
<v Ed McBroom>但我没有——他们没有那样做。这很令人沮丧，因为我和你一样对此感到好奇。

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:18.500
<v Woman 1>Just in talking to you and getting the opposite, you know--
<v Woman 1>只是和你交谈并得到相反的说法，你知道——

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:27.110
<v Ed McBroom>I appreciate you giving us an open ear to it. And certainly, if you have other questions, you hear something else, just call, message me, call the office.
<v Ed McBroom>我很感激你愿意敞开心扉倾听。当然，如果你还有其他问题，听到其他什么，就打电话、给我发消息、打给办公室。

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:30.110
<v Woman 1>Certainly. Well, it's good to hear from you.
<v Woman 1>当然。好吧，很高兴听到你的声音。

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:31.340
<v Ed McBroom>Thank you. Thanks for your time.
<v Ed McBroom>谢谢。谢谢你的时间。

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:32.710
<v Woman 1>Yep.
<v Woman 1>好的。

00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:36.480
<v David Kestenbaum>Can I ask, did it-- does it change anything for you?
<v David Kestenbaum>能问一下吗，它——它对你有什么改变吗？

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:42.080
<v Woman 1>I'm going to go back and reread several things and re-listen to a few podcasts and stuff, and just--
<v Woman 1>我会回去重读几样东西，重新听一些播客之类的，然后只是——

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:43.620
<v Ed McBroom>I hope you'll look up the report.
<v Ed McBroom>我希望你会查阅那份报告。

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:44.480
<v Woman 1>Yeah, I think I will.
<v Woman 1>是的，我想我会的。

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:45.210
<v Ed McBroom>I'd appreciate it.
<v Ed McBroom>我会很感激的。

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:51.380
<v Woman 1>Yeah. Like you said, it's 58 pages. So I'll have to take the time.
<v Woman 1>是的。像你说的那样，它是58页。所以我得花点时间。

00:20:51.380 --> 00:21:02.280
<v David Kestenbaum>It was the furthest I saw him get that day.
<v David Kestenbaum>这是那天我看到他取得的最远进展。

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:13.680
<v David Kestenbaum>Ed tells me he has seen people change their minds. But usually, he learns it because they come back to him later, sometimes with follow-up questions. He wasn't surprised nobody switched sides right in front of us.
<v David Kestenbaum>埃德告诉我，他确实看到过人们改变主意。但通常，他是因为他们稍后回来找他才得知的，有时是带着后续问题。没人就在我们面前倒戈，他并不惊讶。

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:30.940
<v Ed McBroom>You can't expect anybody to just disregard their long-held viewpoint or the narrative that they've accepted as being true. You can't expect them on the spot to really discard that. They need time to think.
<v Ed McBroom>你不能指望任何人直接无视他们长期持有的观点或他们已经接受为真实的叙事。你不能指望他们当场真正摒弃这些。他们需要时间去思考。

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:46.320
<v David Kestenbaum>I pointed out that according to that national poll from earlier in the summer, something like 2/3 of Republicans, or people who lean Republican, believe Biden only won because of voter fraud. Ed says in his experience, it's fewer. But he humors me.
<v David Kestenbaum>我指出，根据那个夏天早些时候的全国民意调查，大约三分之二的共和党人，或倾向共和党的人，认为拜登仅仅是因为选民舞弊才赢的。埃德说，根据他的经验，人数更少。但他顺着我的话说。

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:47.370
<v David Kestenbaum>Can we do the math for a second?
<v David Kestenbaum>我们能算一下数学吗？

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:47.520
<v Ed McBroom>Sure.
<v Ed McBroom>当然。

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:50.090
<v David Kestenbaum>How many people are in your Senate district?
<v David Kestenbaum>你的参议院选区有多少人？

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:54.260
<v Ed McBroom>Oh, around 270-ish, I would say.
<v Ed McBroom>哦，大约27万左右，我会说。

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:57.880
<v David Kestenbaum>Thousand-- OK, how many of those lean Republican?
<v David Kestenbaum>千——好的，其中有多少倾向共和党？

00:21:57.880 --> 00:21:59.410
<v David Kestenbaum>We go with half.
<v David Kestenbaum>我们算一半吧。

00:21:59.410 --> 00:22:09.800
<v David Kestenbaum>That is 90,000 people would need to be convinced. If each conversation is 20 minutes, divide by 3, that's a number of hours.
<v David Kestenbaum>那需要说服9万人。如果每次对话是20分钟，除以3，那是多少小时。

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:19.820
<v Ed McBroom>If you're trying to overwhelm you with the impracticalities of me one at a timing it, you don't have to. I'm already aware of that.
<v Ed McBroom>如果你是想用我一对一的做法不切实际来压倒我，你不必那样做。我已经意识到这一点了。

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:24.390
<v David Kestenbaum>It comes out to 14 years. And that's if it works.
<v David Kestenbaum>算出来是14年。那还得是如果奏效的话。

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:44.190
<v Ira Glass>I was curious if that last woman had talked to had changed her mind at all, the one who had told him, it was nice to hear the other side and was maybe going to go read his report. So I reached out. She said over email she still believed there was election fraud in Michigan, enough to overturn the outcome, and that Ed McBroom was covering it up.
<v Ira Glass>我很好奇那个和他交谈过的最后一个女人是否改变了主意，那个告诉他，很高兴听到另一方的声音、或许要回去读他的报告的人。所以我联系了她。她在电子邮件中说，她仍然相信密歇根州存在选民舞弊，足以推翻结果，而埃德·麦克布鲁姆正在掩盖它。

00:22:44.190 --> 00:23:06.560
<v Ira Glass>This is maybe the best case scenario for changing people's minds on this issue. Like, I do think if anyone could sway people it might be Ed, someone from their own party who also hadn't wanted Biden to win, who explains it all so patiently and clearly. And it's still so hard.
<v Ira Glass>这也许是改变人们在这个问题上的想法的最佳情况了。我是说，我确实认为如果有人能说服人们，那可能是埃德，一个来自他们自己党派、也不希望拜登赢、能如此耐心和清晰地解释这一切的人。而且这仍然如此困难。

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:19.010
<v Ira Glass>Ed reads a lot of American history. I asked him if he worried about the same thing I do. They were so divided. Neither side seems to be able to communicate with each other. No one seems willing to question what they think they know.
<v Ira Glass>埃德读了很多美国历史。我问他是否像我一样担心。他们如此分裂。双方似乎都无法与对方沟通。似乎没有人愿意质疑他们自以为知道的事情。

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:31.440
<v Ira Glass>I asked if he thought we were going off the rails this time. And if what we were going through might be fatal in some way. He said he didn't know. He said the social fabric has been stretched before, and we've gotten through it.
<v Ira Glass>我问他是否认为我们这次要出轨了。以及我们正在经历的事情在某种程度上是否可能是致命的。他说他不知道。他说社会结构以前也曾被拉伸过，我们都挺过来了。

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:40.240
<v Ed McBroom>I hope that that's going to be the case now. But I think there's reasons to have some doubt about that too and to have some trepidation.
<v Ed McBroom>我希望现在也会是这样。但我觉得也有理由对此产生一些怀疑和不安。

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:44.710
<v David Kestenbaum>That keeps me up at night, you know? It really does.
<v David Kestenbaum>这让我彻夜难眠，你知道吗？真的。

00:23:44.710 --> 00:24:00.280
<v Ed McBroom>I understand. I try not to let it worry me, because I firmly believe that we're in God's hands and the nation is in his hands and will trend in the direction that brings him the most glory.
<v Ed McBroom>我明白。我尽量不让它困扰我，因为我坚定地相信我们在上帝的手中，国家也在他的手中，并将向着能带给他最大荣耀的方向发展。

00:24:00.280 --> 00:24:21.830
<v David Kestenbaum>I didn't ask what someone was supposed to hold onto if they don't believe that. Ed's going to keep talking to people. There are lots of issues like this, he says. You just go person to person as long as it takes. That's the job.
<v David Kestenbaum>如果一个人不相信这一点，我没问他应该坚持什么。埃德会继续和人们交谈。他说，有很多像这样的问题。你只要花多长时间就一个人一个人地去谈。这就是工作。

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:29.610
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum is senior editor of our show.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆是我们节目的高级编辑。

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:40.730
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, How I Met My Mother. So one of the producers here at our show, Elna Baker, comes from a long line of passive aggressive communicators. By the way, they know this, they own it.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，我是如何遇见我母亲的。所以我们节目的一位制片人，埃尔娜·贝克，来自一个长期被动攻击型沟通者的家族。顺便说一句，他们知道这一点，他们也承认这一点。

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:54.460
<v Ira Glass>When her grandmother wanted to get a message across, like don't leave change lying around, she put an article on the fridge saying, toddler chokes on penny. And then just leave it to everybody else to connect the dots.
<v Ira Glass>当她祖母想要表达一个信息时，比如不要把零钱乱放，她会把一篇文章贴在冰箱上，写着“幼儿被硬币噎死”。然后就留给其他人去联系上下文了。

00:24:54.460 --> 00:25:07.030
<v Ira Glass>Elna's mother communicates in the same indirect way, which became a problem in the years after Elena left the church. Elna's family's Mormon. And her mom had some important things that she wanted to say to her. Here's Elna.
<v Ira Glass>埃尔娜的母亲以同样间接的方式沟通，这在埃尔娜离开教会后的几年里成了一个问题。埃尔娜的家庭是摩门教徒。而她妈妈有一些想对她说的重要事情。这是埃尔娜。

00:25:07.030 --> 00:25:18.280
<v Elna Baker>This all started when I made a joke about me buying weed to my brother. Careful, he said. If mom were to hear that, she'd send an email to the whole family about how you're an addict. I laughed.
<v Elna Baker>这一切始于我对兄弟开了一个关于我买大麻的玩笑。他轻声说，小心点。如果妈妈听说了，她会给全家发一封电子邮件，说你是个瘾君子。我笑了。

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:26.760
<v Elna Baker>And then I was like, wait, what? That was way too specific. Did mom send an email to the whole family about me being an addict?
<v Elna Baker>然后我就像，等等，什么？这也太具体了。妈妈是不是给全家发了关于我是瘾君子的电子邮件？

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:37.360
<v Elna Baker>And he was like, uh, mm, nothing. Never mind, which could only mean one thing, she did. It took several rounds of questioning before I got the whole story.
<v Elna Baker>他就像，嗯，没什么。算了吧，这只能意味着一件事，她确实发了。我问了几轮，才弄清了全部事实。

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:53.020
<v Elna Baker>The email he was referencing, my mom had sent it 10 years ago. At the time, I was about to host a comedy show in New York, where me and a bunch of performers would play drinking games on stage. It was called The Drunk Show. The show is being promoted online.
<v Elna Baker>他提到的那封邮件，是我妈妈10年前发的。当时，我正要在这个纽约主持一场喜剧秀，我和一群表演者会在舞台上玩喝酒游戏。它叫《醉酒秀》。节目正在网上宣传。

00:25:53.020 --> 00:26:01.420
<v Elna Baker>My mom, a devout Mormon, saw an article about it and freaked out. Mormons don't drink. It's against the religion.
<v Elna Baker>我妈妈，一位虔诚的摩门教徒，看到了一篇关于它的文章，吓坏了。摩门教徒不喝酒。这违背了宗教。

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:19.510
<v Elna Baker>And what I learned from my brother, years after the fact, is that my mom was so worried that I was doing this show, she sent an email to my family, my extended family-- uncles, cousins-- and family friends who I grew up with, like the Mitchells, the Coxes, Heidi, my middle school drama teacher.
<v Elna Baker>而我从兄弟那里了解到，事后多年，我妈妈如此担心我在做这个节目，她给我的家人，我的大家庭——叔叔、表亲——以及我一起长大的家庭朋友，比如米切尔一家、考克斯一家、海蒂，我的中学戏剧老师，发了一封电子邮件。

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:41.890
<v Elna Baker>Dear friends, it begins. I am writing because I am concerned about the direction Elna's life has turned. She is spiraling downward fast. My mom then explains to them that I'm doing this event called The Drunk Show. Then writes, I am very concerned. In our family, you are either an alcoholic or a Mormon. And I think she may be headed in the wrong direction.
<v Elna Baker>“亲爱的朋友们”，它是这样开头的。我写信是因为我担心埃尔娜的生活方向已经改变。她正在快速螺旋式下滑。我妈妈然后向他们解释说我正在做这个叫《醉酒秀》的活动。然后写道，我非常担心。在我们的家庭里，你要么是酒鬼，要么是摩门教徒。我认为她可能走错了方向。

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:52.400
<v Elna Baker>I don't know what I can do personally. She doesn't hear me. I'm going to interject here that I didn't hear her, because she never said anything to me about it.
<v Elna Baker>我不知道我能亲自做什么。她听不进去我的话。我在这里插一句，我没有听她的，因为她从来没有跟我提过这件事。

00:26:52.400 --> 00:27:15.750
<v Elna Baker>In the email, she inserts a link to the article about The Drunk Show and then reveals her grand master plan. She's been commenting on the article under fake names, warning me not to do the show. Can everyone else please make up fake accounts and also comment? This way, I'll cancel the show. She ends, thanks, pray for us both, please.
<v Elna Baker>在邮件里，她插入了那篇关于《醉酒秀》文章的链接，然后透露了她的宏伟计划。她一直在用假名在那篇文章下评论，警告我不要做这个节目。其他人能不能也注册假账户并也进行评论？这样，我就会取消节目。她最后写道，谢谢，请为我们俩祈祷。

00:27:15.750 --> 00:27:27.180
<v Elna Baker>When I read this email, I was mortified. I drink socially. I'm not an alcoholic. But since I'd never intercepted the email, I worried everyone who got it had thought this for years.
<v Elna Baker>当我读到这封邮件时，我感到万分羞愧。我为了社交而喝酒。我不是酒鬼。但因为我从来没有截获过那封邮件，我担心所有收到它的人多年来都一直这样想。

00:27:27.180 --> 00:27:54.010
<v Elna Baker>I immediately clicked on the link to the article. At the bottom of the page, there were four comments from four different people trolling me, all clearly my mother. Her first fake character is Carol from the West Village who says, quote, "encouraging irresponsible drinking that could end in hospitalization of performers is an invitation to a lawsuit. Don't be idiots."
<v Elna Baker>我立刻点击了那篇文章的链接。在页面底部，有来自四个不同人钓鱼式攻击我的评论，所有评论显然都是我母亲。她的第一个假角色是来自西村的卡罗尔，她说，引用：“鼓励不负责任的饮酒可能导致表演者住院，这是在邀请诉讼。别当白痴了。”

00:27:54.010 --> 00:28:08.410
<v Elna Baker>The three other comments escalate from there. Here's the weirdest part. She wrote all these comments while she was staying with me. She was in town the week of The Drunk Show. We slept in the same bed. But she didn't say a word to me about it.
<v Elna Baker>其他三条评论从那里开始升级。这是最奇怪的部分。她在和我住在一起的时候写了所有这些评论。她在《醉酒秀》那一周在城里。我们睡在同一张床上。但她一个字也没跟我提。

00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:29.020
<v Elna Baker>As interventions go, it was the least successful one I've ever heard of or could imagine. No one else among the family or friends chimed in. And I, the target of the intervention, never knew what happened. I never heard of or read the comments online until my brother accidentally let it slip years later.
<v Elna Baker>就干预措施而言，这是我听说过或能想象到的最不成功的一个。家人或朋友中没有人跟进。而我，干预的目标，从来不知道发生了什么。我从来没有听说过或在网上读过那些评论，直到我兄弟几年后不小心说漏了嘴。

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:45.250
<v Elna Baker>Since I found out about this, I've wondered why my mom chose to communicate this message to me the way she did. But I've never asked her, because I figured it would just lead to a fight and no answers. But we're much closer now than we were when I did The Drunk Show. Why not try?
<v Elna Baker>自从我发现了这件事，我就一直想知道为什么我妈妈选择用这种方式向我传达这个信息。但我从来没有问过她，因为我以为这只会导致一场争吵而没有答案。但我们现在比我做《醉酒秀》时亲近多了。为什么不试一试？

00:28:45.250 --> 00:29:02.830
<v Elna Baker>To my surprise, she agreed to talk about it, but on these conditions. Dad had to be there in case we needed mediation. I had to come home for Christmas in exchange. And most importantly, I could only do this story if she got to write the ending, which we'll get to later.
<v Elna Baker>让我惊讶的是，她同意谈谈这件事，但有这些条件。爸爸必须在场，以防我们需要调解。作为交换，我必须回家过圣诞节。而且最重要的是，我只有在她能写结局的情况下才能做这个故事，我们稍后会讲到。

00:29:02.830 --> 00:29:05.200
<v Elna Baker>A talk for a second?
<v Elna Baker>谈一下好吗？

00:29:05.200 --> 00:29:06.490
<v Mom>Hi, Elna, how are you?
<v Mom>嗨，埃尔娜，你好吗？

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:11.200
<v Elna Baker>Wow, mom you sound sexy. Wow, Mom.
<v Elna Baker>哇，妈妈，你的声音听起来很性感。哇，妈妈。

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.860
<v Mom>I'm trying out a lower voice, because I think my voice is too screechy.
<v Mom>我在尝试一种更低沉的声音，因为我觉得我的声音太刺耳了。

00:29:14.860 --> 00:29:19.750
<v Elna Baker>Don't do that. Don't spend the whole interview not being yourself.
<v Elna Baker>别那样。别花整个采访时间不做自己。

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:30.370
<v Ira Glass>We started at the beginning. How did my mother decide that anonymous comments online would be the best way to reach me? She said my sister Julia told her about The Drunk Show.
<v Ira Glass>我们从头开始。我母亲是如何决定网上的匿名评论是联系我的最好方式的？她说我姐姐朱莉告诉她关于《醉酒秀》的事。

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:39.130
<v Mom>So I'm laying there on the couch. I'm at your apartment, probably about 5:00 in the morning. And I'm stewing over this. I can't sleep, having haven't slept for hours.
<v Mom>所以我躺在沙发上。我在你的公寓，大约凌晨5点。我正在为这个心烦意乱。我睡不着，几个小时都没睡着。

00:29:39.130 --> 00:29:52.480
<v Mom>And then I just had this little epiphany. I can put this on here in somebody else's voice. I don't have to use my own voice. And then maybe she'll take it seriously. You just need to hear it from New Yorkers.
<v Mom>然后我突然有了一个小小的顿悟。我可以以别人的声音把它放在上面。我不必用我自己的声音。然后也许她会认真对待。你只需要听到纽约人的意见。

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:53.960
<v Elna Baker>Why?
<v Elna Baker>为什么？

00:29:53.960 --> 00:30:00.050
<v Mom>God, Elna, you're from New York. You live in New York. And you value their opinions.
<v Mom>天哪，埃尔娜，你来自纽约。你住在纽约。你很看重他们的意见。

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:01.490
<v Elna Baker>More than yours?
<v Elna Baker>比看重你的更看重？

00:30:01.490 --> 00:30:13.880
<v Mom>Oh, absolutely, because I'm this fuddy duddy, old fashioned Mormon lady that doesn't know anything. You know, I'm like from 1950. I'm like June Cleaver.
<v Mom>哦，当然，因为我是那种老古板、过时的摩门教女士，什么都不知道。你知道，我就像来自1950年。我就像琼·克利弗。

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:16.760
<v Elna Baker>Are you saying that you are like that or that I think-- you think--
<v Elna Baker>你是说你就是那样，还是说我以为——你以为——

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:19.070
<v Mom>That's what I think you think I am, yeah.
<v Mom>那是我以为你以为我是那样的，是的。

00:30:19.070 --> 00:30:34.880
<v Elna Baker>Before things get too tense, my mom and I pull up the comments page together. The first thing I learned was how fleshed out these people were in my mom's mind, like Carol from the West Village, who warned that irresponsible drinking could lead to lawsuits.
<v Elna Baker>在事情变得太紧张之前，我和妈妈一起打开了评论页面。我学到的第一件事是，这些人在我妈妈心中是多么丰满，比如来自西村的卡罗尔，她警告说不负责任的饮酒可能导致诉讼。

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:39.350
<v Mom>She's a lawyer, because that's the way a lawyer would say things, isn't it?
<v Mom>她是律师，因为那是律师会说话的方式，不是吗？

00:30:39.350 --> 00:30:40.250
<v Elna Baker>OK.
<v Elna Baker>好的。

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:50.780
<v Mom>She's dealt with lawsuits that have involved drunk drivers or something with alcohol is involved, right?
<v Mom>她处理过涉及酒驾或涉及酒精的事情的诉讼，对吧？

00:30:50.780 --> 00:31:01.460
<v Elna Baker>To be clear, nowhere in the comments does it say Carol is a lawyer. For each entry, there's just a name and location, like Don from the Upper West Side.
<v Elna Baker>澄清一下，评论里没有任何地方说卡罗尔是律师。对于每一条，只有一个名字和地点，比如来自上西区的唐。

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:04.670
<v Mom>Pretty ritzy area, right?
<v Mom>挺时髦的地区，对吧？

00:31:04.670 --> 00:31:06.950
<v Elna Baker>Who is done in your mind?
<v Elna Baker>在你心目中唐是谁？

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:09.890
<v Mom>Don is a comedian.
<v Mom>唐是一个喜剧演员。

00:31:09.890 --> 00:31:10.690
<v Elna Baker>Oh, OK.
<v Elna Baker>哦，好的。

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:15.290
<v Mom>Who's older. He used to do kind of the old-style comedy.
<v Mom>谁年纪更大。他过去做那种老派喜剧。

00:31:15.290 --> 00:31:19.460
<v Elna Baker>Again, reading the comments, you'd never know Don was a comedian.
<v Elna Baker>再次重申，阅读评论，你永远不知道唐是喜剧演员。

00:31:19.460 --> 00:31:26.630
<v Mom>Yeah, he's just disgusted with how comedy has evolved in the last 15 years or so.
<v Mom>是的，他只是对喜剧在过去15年左右的发展感到厌恶。

00:31:26.630 --> 00:31:30.740
<v Elna Baker>What does Don think about me doing this show?
<v Elna Baker>唐对我做这个节目有什么看法？

00:31:30.740 --> 00:31:46.940
<v Mom>He says comedy, where you are laughing at the performers and not with the performers is not comedy. It is tragedy. Is this really what NYC comedy is reduced to? Are you really not cleverer than this?
<v Mom>他说喜剧，当你是在嘲笑表演者而不是和表演者一起笑时，那不是喜剧。那是悲剧。这就是纽约喜剧最终变成了这样吗？你真的不比这更聪明吗？

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:53.000
<v Elna Baker>Why did you think, like, Don saying this to me would reach me?
<v Elna Baker>为什么你认为，唐对我这样说会联系到我？

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:57.230
<v Mom>Well, you love comedians. I mean, it's all about the comedy for you.
<v Mom>嗯，你热爱喜剧演员。我是说，这对你来说一切都是关于喜剧。

00:31:57.230 --> 00:31:57.710
<v Elna Baker>Mm-hm.
<v Elna Baker>嗯哼。

00:31:57.710 --> 00:32:04.790
<v Mom>So of course, if this is not going to be funny, then maybe we shouldn't do this.
<v Mom>所以当然，如果这不是好笑的，那么也许我们不应该做这个。

00:32:04.790 --> 00:32:09.920
<v Elna Baker>The next comment comes from the East Village, from a commenter named, Please.
<v Elna Baker>下一条评论来自东村，来自一个叫“拜托”的评论者。

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:15.860
<v Mom>OK, so I'm thinking this is a policeman.
<v Mom>好的，所以我在想这是一个警察。

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:17.690
<v Elna Baker>And his name is Please?
<v Elna Baker>而他的名字叫拜托？

00:32:17.690 --> 00:32:19.520
<v Mom>Yeah, Officer Please.
<v Mom>是的，警官拜托。

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:27.960
<v Elna Baker>It, doesn't say Officer, mom. (LAUGHING) It just says Please.
<v Elna Baker>它上面没写警官，妈妈。（笑）它只是写着拜托。

00:32:27.960 --> 00:32:32.540
<v Elna Baker>Why is this policeman writing me? Why is he so offended by this show?
<v Elna Baker>为什么这个警察要写信给我？为什么他对这个节目如此被冒犯？

00:32:32.540 --> 00:32:37.640
<v Mom>Well, he says 10 years after 9/11, and this is where New York is? Come on.
<v Mom>嗯，他说9/11事件10年后，这就是纽约的样子吗？拜托。

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:40.010
<v Elna Baker>The show was on September 17.
<v Elna Baker>演出是在9月17日。

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:46.820
<v Mom>I think this week of all weeks, we should all be a little more sensitive and full of introspection.
<v Mom>我想这周是所有周中最值得我们大家更加敏感和充满内省的一周。

00:32:46.820 --> 00:32:56.690
<v Elna Baker>OK. Can we just-- I think we can both agree that you went-- you went real big on this one.
<v Elna Baker>好的。我们能只——我觉得我们可以达成共识，你做得——你在这一条上做得太大了。

00:32:56.690 --> 00:32:59.100
<v Mom>I hit the 9/11 button
<v Mom>我按下了9/11按钮。

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:15.500
<v Elna Baker>Her last comment is a straight-up Mormon talking point. Alcoholism, and all other addictions, take away freedom of choice. Seven minutes after she posted that, she sent the mass email. My mom said she went so hard because--
<v Elna Baker>她最后一条评论是一个直截了当的摩门教谈话要点。酗酒，以及所有其他成瘾，夺走了选择的自由。在她发布那条评论七分钟后，她发了那封群发邮件。我妈妈说她做得这么绝是因为——

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:34.230
<v Mom>So I have two uncles who died of alcoholism poisoning. My uncle died at age 30. My other uncle came to my wedding completely drunk and ended up dead about two years later from alcoholism. Gary's grandfather died of alcoholism. So that's how I get there.
<v Mom>所以我有两个叔叔死于酒精中毒。我叔叔死于30岁。我另一个叔叔醉醺醺地来参加我的婚礼，大约两年后死于酗酒。加里的祖父死于酗酒。所以这就是我得出的结论。

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:49.610
<v Mom>It's not like it's a Mormon thing. It's an experience that has affected me. It affected my mother. She was just devastated over her brother and his alcoholism.
<v Mom>这不是像摩门教的事情。这是一个影响了我的经历。它影响了我母亲。她因为她兄弟和他的酗酒而深受打击。

00:33:49.610 --> 00:33:59.300
<v Elna Baker>Stopping The Drunk Show meant stopping me from becoming one of these people. My mom says she saw the promo photo of me sipping a drink on the page announcing the show and thought--
<v Elna Baker>停止《醉酒秀》意味着阻止我成为这些人中的一员。我妈妈说她看到那张我在页面上啜饮饮料的宣传照宣布演出，心想——

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:17.410
<v Mom>Has she lost her mind? She has relatives who died of alcoholism. She's been taught since she was little girl to be careful around alcohol. Has she completely blocked out everything I've ever taught her?
<v Mom>她疯了吗？她有死于酗酒的亲戚。她从小就被教导要小心酒精。她完全屏蔽了所有我教过她的东西吗？

00:34:17.410 --> 00:34:20.930
<v Elna Baker>Did you ever consider just calling me?
<v Elna Baker>你有没有考虑过直接给我打电话？

00:34:20.930 --> 00:34:34.770
<v Mom>Elna, we've already been there. You don't hear me. You just don't hear me. It's just a joke. You have to hear it from somebody who you think is credible.
<v Mom>埃尔娜，我们已经谈过了。你听不进去。你就是听不进去。这只是个玩笑。你得从一个你认为可信的人那里听到。

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:44.320
<v Mom>Come on. Admit it, Elna, you know you would have just laughed. You would have called Kevin. You would have had a good laugh. And you would have had the show anyway.
<v Mom>拜托。承认吧，埃尔娜，你知道你只是会笑笑而已。你会给凯文打电话。你会大笑一场。而且无论如何你都会做那个演出。

00:34:44.320 --> 00:34:45.850
<v Elna Baker>But you didn't-- the thing--
<v Elna Baker>但你没有——那件事——

00:34:45.850 --> 00:34:52.320
<v Mom>Come on. Admit it. You know it. You know you would have rolled your eyes at me.
<v Mom>拜托。承认吧。你知道的。你知道你会对我翻白眼的。

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:54.210
<v Elna Baker>But you didn't even try.
<v Elna Baker>但你甚至没有尝试。

00:34:54.210 --> 00:35:02.280
<v Mom>Elna, just tell me. What's the truth on your end?
<v Mom>埃尔娜，告诉我就行。你的真相是什么？

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:27.340
<v Elna Baker>Well, I don't think I-- here's the thing. I think if you just told me not to do it, I would have done exactly what you're saying. But if we'd had a full conversation where we really talked-- like so for example, I didn't know until just now, until we've had this conversation-- like, I didn't really know that much about your family history with drinking.
<v Elna Baker>嗯，我不觉得我——这是问题所在。我想如果你只是告诉我不要做它，我就会按照你说的做。但如果我们进行了一次充分的对话，我们真正交谈过——比如，我直到现在，直到我们谈话之前，我真的不太了解你的家庭饮酒史。

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:43.330
<v Elna Baker>You don't talk about these unpleasant things. So that like when you're so against drinking, I think I did think it was just had to do with Mormonism and not that it had to do with really painful things you witnessed in your life, because you never told me about them.
<v Elna Baker>你不谈这些不愉快的事情。所以当你如此反对饮酒时，我想我确实认为它只是和摩门教有关，而不是和你在生活中目睹的真正痛苦的事情有关，因为你从来没有告诉过我。

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:55.330
<v Mom>I'm sure I've said this statement before in your life. In our family, you're either Mormon or an--
<v Mom>我相信我以前在你生活中说过这个声明。在我们的家庭里，你要么是摩门教徒，要么是——

00:35:55.330 --> 00:35:55.830
<v Elna Baker>Alcoholic.
<v Elna Baker>酒鬼。

00:35:55.830 --> 00:36:01.360
<v Mom>--alcoholic, yeah. You heard that one before.
<v Mom>——酒鬼，是的。你以前听过那个。

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:04.900
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, I just didn't believe it.
<v Elna Baker>是的，我只是不相信。

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:29.920
<v Ira Glass>She'd said this to me all my life, but I was missing the context. I've never met any of these family members who drank. I don't remember her ever mentioning any of these stories. I saw my mother as sheltered. Almost all her friends are Mormon. I just thought, she doesn't know what she's talking about, which was unfair.
<v Ira Glass>她一生都这样对我说，但我错过了背景。我从来没有见过任何喝酒的家人。我不记得她提过任何这些故事。我看到的母亲是一个受到庇护的人。她所有的朋友几乎都是摩门教徒。我只是想，她不知道她在说什么，这是不公平的。

00:36:29.920 --> 00:36:46.510
<v Ira Glass>When I make a radio story, I listen to my interviews over and over again. And this time, something unusual started to happen. With each listen, my mother sounded less and less like my mother, like I stopped hearing her the way I usually do, rolling my eyes, getting defensive.
<v Ira Glass>当我制作广播故事时，我会一遍又一遍地听我的采访。这一次，一些不寻常的事情开始发生。随着每次聆听，我母亲听起来越来越不像我母亲，就像我停止了通常那样听她说话，翻白眼，变得充满防御性。

00:36:46.510 --> 00:36:52.000
<v Ira Glass>And I actually started to hear what she had to say. This is the line that struck me the hardest.
<v Ira Glass>我实际上开始听到她想说什么。这一行对我触动最大。

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:57.910
<v Mom>Elna, we've already been there. You don't hear me.
<v Mom>埃尔娜，我们已经谈过了。你听不进去。

00:36:57.910 --> 00:37:09.970
<v Elna Baker>She's right. And I can hear in her voice that this has been hurting her feelings for years. And that's my fault. I see why she thinks it's hopeless to talk to me about drinking, about anything.
<v Elna Baker>她是正确的。我能听到她的声音，多年来这一直伤害着她的感情。那是我的错。我明白为什么她认为跟我谈论喝酒、谈论任何事情都是没有希望的。

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:26.680
<v Elna Baker>I'm incredibly dismissive. That's my part in this. And her part, she was totally upfront about that. She didn't want to confront me about drinking, because she has so much trouble with confrontation of any kind with anyone, going back to when she was young.
<v Elna Baker>我非常傲慢。这是我在这件事中的部分。而她的部分，她对此完全坦诚。她不想因为喝酒和我对质，因为她对任何人的任何对质都有很大困难，追溯到她年轻的时候。

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:38.960
<v Mom>I mean, as a little kid, I used to just admit fault to anything that happened in our family in order to get the confrontation to be over with.
<v Mom>我是说，作为一个小孩子，我过去常常承认我们家里发生的任何事情的过错，以结束对质。

00:37:38.960 --> 00:37:51.530
<v Elna Baker>I never understood before this conversation what that feels like for her. She panics, feels trapped, like she needs to run, tightness in her chest, which is what makes this conversation so hard.
<v Elna Baker>在这次谈话之前，我从来不了解这对她来说是什么感觉。她会恐慌，感到被困，就像她需要逃跑，胸口紧绷，这就是这次谈话如此困难的原因。

00:37:51.530 --> 00:37:54.470
<v Mom>This is what-- we're doing exactly what I hate to do.
<v Mom>这就是——我们正在做我最讨厌做的事情。

00:37:54.470 --> 00:37:56.430
<v Elna Baker>What?
<v Elna Baker>什么？

00:37:56.430 --> 00:38:00.580
<v Mom>Talk about conflict.
<v Mom>谈论冲突。

00:38:00.580 --> 00:38:04.030
<v Elna Baker>So what's happening to you when we do it?
<v Elna Baker>那么当我们做这件事时，你身上发生了什么？

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:11.110
<v Mom>Well, see, I'm twiddling my thumbs, right? I'm feeling my neck turn to stone.
<v Mom>嗯，看，我正在玩弄我的大拇指，对吧？我觉得我的脖子变成了石头。

00:38:11.110 --> 00:38:20.890
<v Dad>This cord, which is already coiled up, has been triple coiled, for the headphones.
<v Dad>这根电线，已经盘起来了，为了耳机，被盘了三圈。

00:38:20.890 --> 00:38:23.720
<v Mom>So, yeah.
<v Mom>所以，是的。

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:31.040
<v Elna Baker>Well, thank you for putting yourself in your most uncomfortable place.
<v Elna Baker>嗯，谢谢你把你放在你最不舒服的地方。

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:34.900
<v Mom>Sure. You're welcome, Elna, anything for my daughter.
<v Mom>当然。不客气，埃尔娜，为了我的女儿什么都愿意做。

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:57.110
<v Elna Baker>I knew what this meant. From the tone of her voice, it was time to stop. This was the most direct conversation I've ever had with my mother, and the longest real conversation we've ever had. She'd gone above and beyond and did something she did not enjoy for me. I could return the favor and call it a day.
<v Elna Baker>我知道这意味着什么。从她声音的语调来看，是时候停止了。这是我和我母亲进行过的最直接的谈话，也是我们进行过的最长的真正谈话。她超越了自我，做了一些她不喜欢但为了我而做的事情。我可以礼尚往来，到此为止。

