00:49:05.800 --> 00:49:29.120
<v Jeanne Darst>And Ernie the Attorney's face pops up off the screen again, along with his three kids identified as my half nieces and nephew. I have no idea why confirming Ernest as my brother confirms my father is my father, but it just does. The question now is, do we contact this sweet-looking, innocent 62-year-old man and overturn everything he believes about his identity?
<v Jeanne Darst>而恩尼律师的脸再次从屏幕上跳了出来，连同他的三个被确认为我半侄女和侄子的孩子。我不知道为什么确认恩尼是我的兄弟证实了我的父亲是我的父亲，但这确实是这样。现在的问题是，我们要联系这个看起来甜美、无辜的62岁男人并推翻他所相信的关于他身份的一切吗？

00:49:29.120 --> 00:49:41.150
<v Jeanne Darst>We decide to go for it. And I email him saying, I believe you're my half brother. And then Liz and I act like crazy people for the next 24 hours until he emails back. And the next day, we're meeting for the first time on Zoom.
<v Jeanne Darst>我们决定去做。我发邮件给他，说，我相信你是我的同父异母兄弟。然后利兹和我就像疯子一样在接下来的24小时里等待他回信。第二天，我们第一次通过Zoom见面。

00:49:41.150 --> 00:49:55.220
<v Jeanne Darst>He tells us that his parents got divorced when he was five and there was a big custody battle. And when he was 17, his mother, in a fit of boozy despair, blurted out that his father was not his father, that his father was a man named Stephen Darst, who is a writer from St. Louis.
<v Jeanne Darst>他告诉我们，他的父母在他五岁时离婚了，有一场大的监护权争夺战。在他17岁时，他的母亲，在一次酒醉的绝望中，脱口而出他的父亲不是他的父亲，他的父亲是一个叫斯蒂芬·达斯特的人，一个来自圣路易斯的作家。

00:49:55.220 --> 00:50:08.090
<v Jeanne Darst>And then Ernest, raised by a psychoanalyst, says he considered the information and had no idea what to do with it, and promptly repressed it. And no one ever talked about it after that day. He says he never thought of it again until I emailed him.
<v Jeanne Darst>然后恩尼，由一位心理分析家抚养长大，说他考虑了这些信息，不知道该怎么做，并迅速压制了它。从那天以后再没有人谈论过它。他说他从来没再想过这件事，直到我给他发了邮件。

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:22.500
<v Jeanne Darst>One weird coincidence, he tells us that for years, his father dated a woman who lived in Bronxville, New York. And they would go visit her all the time in Bronxville, where we lived. We could have run into him at the A&P.
<v Jeanne Darst>一个奇怪的巧合，他告诉我们多年来，他的父亲和一个住在纽约布朗克斯维尔的女人约会。他们总是去布朗克斯维尔看望她，而我们就住在那里。我们本可能在A&P超市遇到他。

00:50:22.500 --> 00:50:49.890
<v Jeanne Darst>The following week, Liz goes to my dad's and helps him get on a Zoom with everybody. And suddenly, I'm watching my 87-year-old father meet his 62-year-old son. And I'm seeing Ernie get information about his mother from my dad, who remembers absolutely every detail about everything-- the restaurants they went to, how she called her dog Daisy "Desi" in her accent, how every time they would part they would walk about 100 feet in opposite directions, and my father would turn around and yell, hey, Raqui.
<v Jeanne Darst>接下来的一周，利兹去了我爸爸那里，帮他和一个Zoom和所有人连上。突然，我看着我87岁的父亲遇见他62岁的儿子。我看着恩尼从我爸爸那里得到关于他母亲的信息，爸爸对一切细节都记忆犹新——他们去过的餐厅，她用口音把她的狗戴西叫成“德西”，每次他们分开时他们都会向相反方向走大约100英尺，然后我父亲会转过身大喊，嘿，拉奎。

00:50:49.890 --> 00:51:14.360
<v Jeanne Darst>And when she turned around, my dad would make this embracing gesture with his arms. Earnest says to me, it's amazing to talk about my mom with a man who loved her, which Swedish dad did not. Ernie sends us pictures of Raqui. She looks like a Panamanian Sophia Loren. In fact, Raqui was a lot like my mom. She was beautiful, sort of fancy. Her grandfather was the president of Panama.
<v Jeanne Darst>当她转身时，爸爸会做这种用双臂拥抱的姿势。恩尼对我说，和爱过她的人谈论我的妈妈真是太神奇了，而瑞典爸爸却没有。恩尼发给我们拉奎的照片。她看起来像巴拿马的索菲亚·罗兰。事实上，拉奎和我的妈妈很像。她很漂亮，有点时髦。她的祖父是巴拿马总统。

00:51:14.360 --> 00:51:28.890
<v Jeanne Darst>She drank too much, smoked too much, and had major depression. My dad definitely has a type. The whole thing with Ernie was fun and exciting. My sisters and I were talking on the phone all the time, you know, the three of us that are currently speaking to each other.
<v Jeanne Darst>她喝得太多，抽得太多，并且有严重的抑郁症。爸爸确实有喜欢的人选。关于恩尼的整件事有趣又令人兴奋。我和姐妹们一直都在打电话，你知道，我们三个目前还在和对方说话的姐妹。

00:51:28.890 --> 00:51:43.140
<v Jeanne Darst>But also what made it so exciting was how together an "adulty" he was. He sent me a card with a watercolor drawing of his house in New Orleans on the front, which when I got it, I was like, oh, my God, he's got stationery. None of us girls have stationery.
<v Jeanne Darst>但同样让它如此令人兴奋的是他表现得多么得体和“成人”。他寄给我一张卡片，正面有他纽奥良房子的水彩画，当我拿到它时，我心想，哦，我的天，他有信笺。我们这些女孩都没有信笺。

00:51:43.140 --> 00:51:51.660
<v Jeanne Darst>He's decent and caring and even-tempered. He asks me how I am and how I feel about this or that. And then he waits for me to answer.
<v Jeanne Darst>他得体、有爱心且脾气温和。他问我怎么样，我对这个或那个感觉如何。然后他等着我回答。

00:51:51.660 --> 00:52:07.720
<v Jeanne Darst>He made me Raqui's Panamanian Christmas cookies. He was kind, not something in great supply in my showboating drinking, dancy family. Kind is what my mother would have said about people who couldn't throw a decent cocktail party. People who just weren't with it.
<v Jeanne Darst>他给我做了拉奎的巴拿马圣诞饼干。他很善良，这在我那个炫耀、喝酒、跳舞的家庭里并不多见。善良是妈妈会用在那些不能办一场体面的鸡尾酒会的人身上的词。那些只是没跟上节奏的人。

00:52:07.720 --> 00:52:32.040
<v Jeanne Darst>I started to feel like this was our chance to be a bona fide loving family. So Liz and I had a plan to throw an 88th birthday party for our dad and lure Ernie and his wife Donna to New York for it. We never actually said this to each other, but the big question in the air was, is this going to be a real relationship? Is this just going to be a few Zoom calls, or are we going to get together at holidays with our kids for years to come?
<v Jeanne Darst>我开始觉得这是我们成为真正相亲相爱家庭的机会。所以利兹和我计划为爸爸举办88岁生日派对，并引诱恩尼和他的妻子唐娜来纽约参加。我们从来没对彼此真正说过，但空气中巨大的问题是，这会是真正的关系吗？这只是几次Zoom通话，还是我们未来几年会在节日里带着孩子聚在一起？

00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:43.780
<v Jeanne Darst>We'd get together in person and Liz's backyard to find out. You know, no pressure.
<v Jeanne Darst>我们会亲自在利兹的后院聚在一起找出答案。你知道，没压力。

00:52:43.780 --> 00:53:07.150
<v Jeanne Darst>Since Ernie is a lot more normal than we are, after he accepts the invitation to come and meet all of us, Liz and I agree we need to get our curb appeal together, just a light retouching of a few family dramas, just price out some smoke and a few mirrors. I mean, should we say mom died of alcoholism in a West Village [BLEEP] with mice running around? Or should we just say she was a rich girl from St. Louis, who was a champion equestrian and debutante?
<v Jeanne Darst>既然恩尼比我们正常得多，在他接受邀请来见我们所有人之后，利兹和我同意我们需要整理好我们的门面，只是对一些家庭戏剧进行轻微的润色，只是给一些烟雾和几个镜子标个价。我是说，我们应该说妈妈死于酒精中毒，住在一个有老鼠跑来跑去的西村[哔]里吗？还是我们应该说她是个来自圣路易斯的富家女孩，是冠军马术家和名媛？

00:53:07.150 --> 00:53:39.300
<v Jeanne Darst>And do we mention that Amy married a Tunisian man and took off to his family's farm in Tunisia and didn't speak to any of us for eight years but is now back and never really explained any of this to us? The real worry though is, of course, my father. How do we prevent Ernie from seeing that while my father is the ideal person to sit next to at a bar-- funny, a million stories, a truly entertaining charismatic man-- those aren't the most important qualities in a father? He's painfully unaware of other people's needs and feelings.
<v Jeanne Darst>我们提到艾米嫁给了一个突尼斯男人并带着他去了他家在突尼斯的农场，八年没和我们说话，但现在回来了，也从未真正向我们解释过这些吗？真正的担心当然是我父亲。我们如何阻止恩尼看到，虽然爸爸是酒吧里最理想的谈话对象——有趣、有百万个故事、一个真正有趣又有魅力的人——但这些不是父亲身上最重要的品质。他对别人的需求和感受毫无察觉。

00:53:39.300 --> 00:54:02.800
<v Jeanne Darst>And when you really do need him, he's just not able to be there for you in the way that you want him to be. Like, when my mother died of a stroke after years of really grim alcoholism, he offered no words of comfort, but instead asked me what we should do with the bloody couch that she died on. Should I go over to mama's and take that bloody couch out of view so you girls don't have to see it? Although, where the hell am I going to drag a bloody couch? I'll probably get arrested.
<v Jeanne Darst>当你真正需要他时，他就是没法以你想要的方式陪伴你。比如，我母亲在多年的痛苦酗酒后死于中风时，他没有提供任何安慰的话，而是问我我们应该怎么处理她死在上面的那张该死的沙发？我要不要去妈妈那里把那张该死的沙发从视线中移走，这样你们女孩就不必看到了？虽然，我到底要把那张该死的沙发拖到哪里去？我可能会被逮捕。

00:54:02.800 --> 00:54:21.660
<v Jeanne Darst>Is seeing that bloody couch going to traumatize you girls, do you think? When I tried to get him to talk about what he was feeling or take some interest in what I was feeling, he wasn't mean about it. He's not a mean person. It's more like he just can't do it, like he'd gently reroute the conversation in a different direction.
<v Jeanne Darst>看到那张该死的沙发会创伤你们女孩吗，你们觉得？当我试图让他谈论他的感受或对我的感受产生兴趣时，他并不刻薄。他不是个刻薄的人。更像是他根本做不到，就像他会温柔地把谈话引向另一个方向。

00:54:21.660 --> 00:54:43.940
<v Jeanne Darst>Get him on the phone, and within two minutes, he launches into a steroid-fueled monologue about his obsession with the beginnings of the neocon movement or with Russian ballerina Diana Vishneyva. And once he's off and running, there's no interrupting him. You just wander in and out of the room with him on speaker, doing your laundry, making dinner, because he can go on forever. You just have to make a decision about when you can no longer go on.
<v Jeanne Darst>通过电话打给他，两分钟之内，他就启动了一段关于他迷恋新保守主义运动的开始或者是迷恋俄罗斯芭蕾舞演员戴安娜·维什涅娃的类固醇驱动的独白。一旦他开始，就没人能打断他。你只是对他开着免提在他房间里进进出出，洗衣服，做饭，因为他可以永远讲下去。你只是得决定你什么时候不能再继续了。

00:54:43.940 --> 00:55:02.370
<v Jeanne Darst>And so I guess I'm worried about Ernie's big day. The day of the party was extremely humid and buggy in my sister's backyard in Brooklyn. And rain threatened to force us inside, where it would be very cramped.
<v Jeanne Darst>所以我猜我在担心恩尼的大日子。派对当天，我在布鲁克林我姐姐的后院，天气非常潮湿且有很多虫子。雨威胁要迫使我们进去，那里会非常挤。

00:55:02.370 --> 00:55:17.910
<v Jeanne Darst>Seeing my father next to Ernie for the first time was mind-bending they looked so much alike. After some hugs and pictures, we all sit at the long outside table. I'm welling up. I can't imagine how emotional this must be for Ernie. His wife Donna is squeezing his hand in support.
<v Jeanne Darst>第一次看到父亲和恩尼站在一起真是不可思议，他们长得太像了。经过一些拥抱和照片，我们都坐在长长的外桌旁。我热泪盈眶。我无法想象这对恩尼来说一定多么情绪化。他的妻子唐娜正在紧紧握住他的手以示支持。

00:55:17.910 --> 00:55:28.650
<v Jeanne Darst>I look around the table and wonder, why is Ernie not sitting next to my dad? Shouldn't my dad have said, Ernie, sit next to me? It's like he forgets he's the dad.
<v Jeanne Darst>我环顾餐桌，心想，为什么恩尼没有坐在爸爸旁边？爸爸不应该说，恩尼，坐在我旁边吗？就像他忘记了他是爸爸。

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:40.600
<v Jeanne Darst>But very quickly, we all get talking and laughing. And Ernie gives my dad a picture of Raqui for his birthday. And it feels like the whole thing is going great. Then the rain comes down, and we hustle inside, and we're fumbling to get back to that initial connection.
<v Jeanne Darst>但很快，我们都开始谈话大笑。恩尼给爸爸一份拉奎尔的照片作为生日礼物。感觉这一切进行得非常棒。然后大雨倾盆，我们匆忙跑进去，我们在试图回到最初的联系中笨手笨脚。

00:55:40.600 --> 00:56:07.890
<v Jeanne Darst>Then the rain stops. And we're back in the yard, eating, drinking. Down the table, I see my father, now sitting next to Ernie. But Ernie hasn't said anything for a really long time. So I know my dad is definitely talking about F. Scott Fitzgerald and his theory that Fitzgerald forced his wife Zelda to have an abortion at the Plaza Hotel in 1922, a topic which has dominated our lives for decades. For as long as I can remember, it's the number one thing he uses to avoid talking about anything real.
<v Jeanne Darst>然后雨停了。我们回到院子里，吃着、喝着。在桌子的那一头，我看到父亲现在坐在恩尼旁边。但恩尼已经很长时间没说话了。所以我知道爸爸绝对是在谈论F·斯科特·菲茨杰拉德和他关于菲茨杰拉德在1922年在广场酒店强迫妻子塞尔达流产的理论，这个话题占据了我们几十年的生活。只要我记得，这就是他用来避免谈论任何真实事情的头号事情。

00:56:07.890 --> 00:56:30.420
<v Jeanne Darst>When my son was four weeks old, and my husband and I were about to move to California, he said he was stopping by with a present for the baby, which turned out to be some Stilton from Murray's Cheese shop. And as I hobbled around post c-section, packing our suitcases and breastfeeding the baby, completely about to fall apart, he nibbled on the blue cheese and talked about Zelda Fitzgerald's abortion and its aftermath.
<v Jeanne Darst>当我儿子四周大时，我丈夫和我要搬去加利福尼亚，他说他要过来给婴儿送个礼物，结果是从默里奶酪店带的一些斯蒂尔顿奶酪。当我剖腹产后跛着脚，收拾行李和母乳喂养孩子，完全快要崩溃时，他嚼着蓝色奶酪，谈论着塞尔达·菲茨杰拉德的堕胎及其余波。

00:56:30.420 --> 00:56:42.780
<v Jeanne Darst>Liz and I had strategized before the party that if he got on Fitzgerald, we would cut him off before it could get going. But it's too late now. I'm horrified, because I don't think my dad has asked Ernie anything about him.
<v Jeanne Darst>利兹和我在派对前制定了策略，如果他谈论菲茨杰拉德，我们就会在他开始之前切断他。但现在已经太晚了。我很恐惧，因为我不觉得爸爸问过恩尼任何关于他的事情。

00:56:42.780 --> 00:57:12.890
<v Jeanne Darst>Does he honestly have zero curiosity about this person, this child whom he almost missed entirely? Ernie looks bewildered and heartbroken. Watching a 62-year-old man get crushed right in front of me, watching him wait patiently to be seen by a man whose eyes are looking elsewhere, it was like watching my spirit get crushed, like a reenactment.
<v Jeanne Darst>他真的对这个人、这个孩子一点好奇心都没有吗，他差点完全错过的孩子？恩尼看起来困惑又心碎。看着一个62岁的男人就在我面前被粉碎，看着他耐心等待着被一个眼睛看向别处的男人所看见，这就像看着我的灵魂被粉碎，就像一次重演。

00:57:12.890 --> 00:57:25.850
<v Jeanne Darst>My father produces a fountain pen that Raqui gave him. It's engraved with his initials. Ernest looks really excited. But my father isn't saying, this is for you Ernest. I want you to have this.
<v Jeanne Darst>父亲拿出了拉奎尔给他的钢笔。上面刻着他的名字首字母。恩尼看起来非常兴奋。但父亲没有说，这是给你的恩尼。我想要你拥有这个。

00:57:25.850 --> 00:57:44.750
<v Jeanne Darst>We all wait for him to say that. But instead, he says, now, should I hang on to this and give it to you later or now? I yell, now, Dad. The metal tip is slightly bent. It doesn't work. But you have to hand it to my dad for simply not losing it for over half a century.
<v Jeanne Darst>我们都在等他那样说。但他反而说，现在，我是应该留着它还是以后给你？我大喊，现在，爸爸。金属尖稍微有点弯。它没法用。但你不得不佩服我爸爸只是没把它弄丢了半个多世纪。

00:57:44.750 --> 00:57:57.290
<v Jeanne Darst>He gives it to Ernie. Ernie looks thrilled and says, I know a guy in New Orleans who can fix it.
<v Jeanne Darst>他把它给了恩尼。恩尼看起来很激动说，我知道纽奥良有个家伙能修好它。

00:57:57.290 --> 00:58:11.780
<v Jeanne Darst>In my post-mortem with my dad a few days later, he talks about the food, about my niece's pretty dress, talks about all kinds of things. But I have to push him really hard to say anything about meeting his son. I'm practically mugging him for an emotion.
<v Jeanne Darst>几天后在我和爸爸的“验尸”谈话中，他谈到了食物，谈到了我侄女漂亮的裙子，谈到了各种事情。但我不得不强迫他去说任何关于遇见他儿子的事情。我简直是在向他抢劫一种情绪。

00:58:11.780 --> 00:58:24.180
<v Jeanne Darst>Finally he says, I think I'll have a great relationship with Ernie. And I thank you very much for that, Jean-Joe, for bringing us together. So much for my dream of Ernie transforming us into a more loving family.
<v Jeanne Darst>最后他说，我想我会和恩尼建立伟大的关系。我非常感谢你做这些，让-乔，把我们聚在一起。这就是我梦想恩尼把我们变成更相亲相爱的家庭的结局。

00:58:24.180 --> 00:58:39.840
<v Jeanne Darst>Let's be honest. My family is like a long-running production of The Fantasticks, where all the actors are too old for the characters they're playing and the lighting people miss a third of the cues. But now, there's this one fresh faced kid just out of drama school who brings down the house in every scene.
<v Jeanne Darst>老实说。我的家庭就像长久上演的《幻想曲》，所有演员对于他们扮演的角色都太老了，灯光师错过了三分之一的提示。但现在，有个刚从戏剧学院出来的清新脸孔的孩子在每一场戏里都赢得了满堂彩。

00:58:39.840 --> 00:58:59.790
<v Jeanne Darst>Ernie sends an email after the party where he describes our family as, having a bond I truly admire, and that he felt included in a way that felt genuine and from the heart. Wow, Liz says, we really put on a hell of a show. And then Liz and I have a Zoom with Ernie, Ernest, Ernest Enrique Svenson.
<v Jeanne Darst>恩尼在派对后发了一封邮件，他形容我们的家庭是，“有着一种我真正钦佩的纽带”，而且他感到一种发自内心、真实的方式被包容。哇，利兹说，我们真的上演了一场精彩的表演。然后利兹和我就和恩尼，恩尼斯特，恩尼斯特·恩里克·斯文森进行了Zoom。

00:58:59.790 --> 00:59:12.390
<v Jeanne Darst>Steve sent me something after the weekend, he says. What, we ask? 117 page transcript of Zelda and F. Scott's therapy sessions that Steve hand copied at the Princeton archives. Have you seen it, he asks?
<v Jeanne Darst>周末后史蒂夫寄给我一些东西，他说。什么，我们问？117页的塞尔达和F·斯科特的治疗会议笔录，史蒂夫在普林斯顿档案馆手抄的。你见过了吗，他问？

00:59:12.390 --> 00:59:37.860
<v Jeanne Darst>Oh, Ernie, have we seen it? Yes, we have, we say, not sure how much to give away. He's got a long road ahead. And he's got to walk it just like we did. It's pretty interesting, Ernie says. Yeah, we say, yeah, it is.
<v Jeanne Darst>哦，恩尼，我们见过吗？是的，我们见过，我们说，不确定要透露多少。他还有很长的路要走。而且他必须像我们一样走过它。这很有趣，恩尼说。是的，我们说，是的，很有趣。

00:59:37.860 --> 00:59:42.360
<v Ira Glass>Jeanne Darst, she's currently developing her book, Fiction Ruined My Family for television.
<v Ira Glass>让·达斯特，她目前正在为电视开发她的书《小说毁了我的家庭》。

